will demonstrate...

260
00:10:03,367 --> 00:10:06,000
(gurgling)

261
00:10:08,333 --> 00:10:10,133
It looks easy, but
actually they are prone

262
00:10:10,167 --> 00:10:11,767
to catching edges, which
would cause a spill.

263
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:14,233
Greater speed is needed
for this stunt.

264
00:10:14,267 --> 00:10:16,567
Man, imagine what he could
do with a full set,

265
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:17,800
a full size, huh?

266
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:20,767
Here's Alfredo Mendoza,

267
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:22,333
one of the greats in
water skiing.

268
00:10:22,367 --> 00:10:23,367
I know his skis.

269
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:25,267
I'm gonna sniff myself!

270
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:27,100
He doesn't need skis.

271
00:10:27,133 --> 00:10:28,933
His bare feet will do nicely.

272
00:10:28,967 --> 00:10:30,767
Oh, he's got webbed toes.

273
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:31,733
He says it's easy

274
00:10:31,767 --> 00:10:32,367
if you don't wiggle your toes.

275
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:33,667
Am I a clown?

276
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:35,333
Do you find me amusing?

277
00:10:36,333 --> 00:10:38,700
There's only one way to stop:
just let go.

278
00:10:38,733 --> 00:10:40,567
Of your sensibility and
dress like a girl!

279
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:41,767
You know you want to.

280
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:43,300
This young lady is
showing us the type

281
00:10:43,333 --> 00:10:45,667
of ski used for the
single ski technique.

282
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:47,367
That's Alfredo, campin' it up!

283
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:50,067
Next is the regulation
or two ski type.

284
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:52,433
It has less depth to the
fin on the bottom.

285
00:10:52,467 --> 00:10:54,567
And she has less depth as well.

286
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:55,633
Hey, hey.

287
00:10:55,667 --> 00:10:56,867
Oops.

288
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:58,033
And the third is
wider with no fin.

289
00:10:58,067 --> 00:10:59,433
She looks fine to me.

290
00:10:59,467 --> 00:11:00,567
It is used for
turning and jumping.

291
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:02,433
Hey fellas, are you listening?

292
00:11:02,467 --> 00:11:05,500
It's the way she touches
that board, it's...

293
00:11:05,533 --> 00:11:08,067
Single ski technique,
while different,

294
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:09,667
is just as easy to perform.

295
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:11,633
It has more grace and style.

296
00:11:11,667 --> 00:11:12,700
I can see that.

297
00:11:12,733 --> 00:11:14,700
Allows the skier to operate
at a wider range.

298
00:11:14,733 --> 00:11:15,833
It's just a whole lot better,
okay?

299
00:11:15,867 --> 00:11:16,700
Trust me!

300
00:11:16,733 --> 00:11:18,200
We're sold.

301
00:11:19,533 --> 00:11:20,733
Let's watch Willa McGuire.

302
00:11:20,767 --> 00:11:22,167
Let's watch Willa Cather

303
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:23,933
doing her baby
schnooks impression.

304
00:11:23,967 --> 00:11:26,767
Her artistry and
skill has no equal

305
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:27,967
on the aquatic hickory.

306
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:28,933
Hickory?

307
00:11:28,967 --> 00:11:30,467
Oh, it's Leona Helmsley!

308
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:32,267
Taxes are for the little people.

309
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:34,967
Harry, Harry, come get me up.

310
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:43,533
I'm building the master race.

311
00:11:43,567 --> 00:11:44,700
Willa tries the impossible.

312
00:11:44,733 --> 00:11:47,367
A ski ballet with no
hand or foot hold.

313
00:11:52,133 --> 00:11:54,433
She should be home caring
for her family,

314
00:11:54,467 --> 00:11:56,100
but no, I'll do everything.

315
00:11:56,133 --> 00:11:57,867
You just keep up your
little ski ballet

316
00:11:57,900 --> 00:11:59,733
or whatever the heck
it is you do.

317
00:12:00,767 --> 00:12:01,767
Nice.

318
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:03,633
A popular form of
modern water skiing

319
00:12:03,667 --> 00:12:05,700
is sometimes called the
romantic double.

320
00:12:05,733 --> 00:12:07,433
Hey, you come here often?

321
00:12:07,467 --> 00:12:09,967
Hey, kick those skis off,
gimme your foot.

322
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,233
There you go.

323
00:12:11,267 --> 00:12:12,933
Plenty of room on top,
here you go.

324
00:12:12,967 --> 00:12:16,033
You are friendly, aren't you.
(chuckles)

325
00:12:16,067 --> 00:12:17,767
Well, just throw that
stuff in the back,

326
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:20,267
I kind of live off my shoulders.

327
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:21,767
Wee!

328
00:12:22,933 --> 00:12:24,433
Hey hey hey, get a boat.

329
00:12:24,467 --> 00:12:25,900
I mean, get a room.

330
00:12:25,933 --> 00:12:29,567
Now the two young lovers bask
in the afterglow.

331
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:31,267
How about a kiss?

332
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:33,533
No?

333
00:12:33,567 --> 00:12:34,267
No?

334
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:35,467
Goodbye.

335
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:36,900
Hah, you're shark food now,
baby!

336
00:12:36,933 --> 00:12:38,100
Oh that's nice.

337
00:12:38,133 --> 00:12:39,533
Here's hoping this romantic pair

338
00:12:39,567 --> 00:12:40,700
have a happier ending.

339
00:12:40,733 --> 00:12:41,833
See, she's doing it!

340
00:12:41,867 --> 00:12:43,300
Yeah!

341
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:48,467
They gotta watch out, this
could lead to dancing.

342
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:50,700
Bob and Connie
really enjoy life.

343
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:54,633
Hey, victory at sea, Bob.

344
00:12:54,667 --> 00:12:55,700
Woo woo!

345
00:12:55,733 --> 00:12:57,033
Alfredo is warming up again.

346
00:12:57,067 --> 00:12:58,633
This time, it's for the jump.

347
00:12:58,667 --> 00:13:01,100
He holds the world's record of
well over a hundred feet

348
00:13:01,133 --> 00:13:02,400
in board jumping.

349
00:13:03,967 --> 00:13:05,700
I'm bored jumping.

350
00:13:05,733 --> 00:13:07,333
Let's watch him again
in slow motion.

351
00:13:07,367 --> 00:13:08,767
Notice the arms do not rotate

352
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:10,667
like on snow ski jumping.

353
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:12,867
They just snap clean away!

354
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:17,767
(classical music)

355
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:21,267
Oh, all of a sudden it's Sergia
Prokofiev playing.

356
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:24,033
Thought it was Handel's Water
Music. (chuckles)

357
00:13:24,067 --> 00:13:26,233
Alfredo's next is called the
helicopter spin.

358
00:13:26,267 --> 00:13:28,467
He'll try to do a double spin.

359
00:13:28,500 --> 00:13:30,167
It's never been done before

360
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:32,067
and it takes nerve to try.

361
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:33,767
Oh, I can do that.

362
00:13:37,467 --> 00:13:38,267
Whoa.

363
00:13:38,900 --> 00:13:40,767
Almost, but not quite.

364
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:42,900
Remember that agony of
defeat guy on ABC?

365
00:13:42,933 --> 00:13:44,567
That was him in the
summer months.

366
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:45,767
Oh, he's summering.

367
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:47,300
This time the Mexican
jumping bean

368
00:13:47,333 --> 00:13:48,700
will do a one and a half
spin on the ramp,

369
00:13:48,733 --> 00:13:51,833
landing backwards and completing
the other half on water.

370
00:13:51,867 --> 00:13:53,700
Now this time, the white
fascist narrator

371
00:13:53,733 --> 00:13:55,367
will make a racial slur!

372
00:13:56,733 --> 00:13:58,167
Oh, but what do I know?

373
00:13:58,200 --> 00:13:59,467
I'm only a fat pink announcer

374
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:01,167
mowing down pretzels and
pinwheel cookies

375
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:03,300
and trying to come to grips
with the tattered ends

376
00:14:03,333 --> 00:14:05,633
of a once promising life
gone horribly wrong.

377
00:14:05,667 --> 00:14:08,233
God, god, why? (sobs)

378
00:14:08,267 --> 00:14:09,800
It's just a movie.

379
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:15,533
Alfredo doesn't like to
get his feet wet

380
00:14:15,567 --> 00:14:16,467
coming ashore.

381
00:14:16,500 --> 00:14:19,600
(plane noises)

382
00:14:20,867 --> 00:14:23,933
The triple straight jump
has crowd appeal.

383
00:14:25,733 --> 00:14:27,833
Once more for the cameraman.

384
00:14:27,867 --> 00:14:29,933
One for my baby and one more,
for the cameraman.

385
00:14:29,967 --> 00:14:31,333
This is the shot he was after.

386
00:14:31,367 --> 00:14:32,733
Where's the third fellow?

387
00:14:32,767 --> 00:14:35,067
He's chicken, never
jumped at all.

388
00:14:35,100 --> 00:14:36,333
What's this?

389
00:14:36,367 --> 00:14:37,867
He's forced his way into the
announcer's booth!

390
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:38,933
What's that in his hand?

391
00:14:38,967 --> 00:14:39,967
Oh no, ah!

392
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,867
(gun noises)

393
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:44,233
Believe it or not, even
water skiers get down

394
00:14:44,267 --> 00:14:46,233
with that old bugaboo monotony.

395
00:14:46,267 --> 00:14:48,700
So they choke each other
with their tow ropes.

396
00:14:48,733 --> 00:14:49,933
(choking noises)

397
00:14:49,967 --> 00:14:51,167
When that happens,

398
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:52,700
the boys play a little game.

399
00:14:52,733 --> 00:14:55,133
They call it the
Cypress Garden weave.

400
00:14:55,167 --> 00:14:57,467
(chuckles) They call it
the sure death.

401
00:14:57,500 --> 00:14:58,867
Well I hope they're
all pepped up,

402
00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:00,033
because I'm exhausted.

403
00:15:00,067 --> 00:15:02,867
Yes, waterskiing today
is a big sport,

404
00:15:02,900 --> 00:15:05,067
and here is the big sendoff
from the whole gang.

405
00:15:05,100 --> 00:15:06,767
V is for Victory!

406
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:09,233
Buy war bonds where you
work or bank!

407
00:15:09,267 --> 00:15:10,100
Aw.

408
00:15:10,900 --> 00:15:14,100
(fun futuristic music)

409
00:15:16,967 --> 00:15:17,967
(old-timey music)

410
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:18,967
♪ Come along with me

411
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:20,233
♪ Maybe then you'll see

412
00:15:20,267 --> 00:15:22,033
♪ My harnesses and my nets

413
00:15:22,067 --> 00:15:23,967
♪ Hey, catching trouble,
you and me

414
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,067
♪ Catching trouble,
baby you can't see

415
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:28,133
♪ Catching trouble and
maybe a kiss or three

416
00:15:28,167 --> 00:15:29,733
(whistles)

417
00:15:30,300 --> 00:15:31,867
Catching Trouble?

418
00:15:31,900 --> 00:15:33,133
Hello everybody, this
is Ted Husing,

419
00:15:33,167 --> 00:15:35,233
and I'm very happy to again
present Sport Light's

420
00:15:35,267 --> 00:15:36,733
good friend Ross Allen

421
00:15:36,767 --> 00:15:37,900
Hi, Ross!

422
00:15:37,933 --> 00:15:39,167
A modern Tarzan.

423
00:15:39,200 --> 00:15:40,533
Oh, he's going golfing.

424
00:15:40,567 --> 00:15:42,067
Leaving his headquarters,

425
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:43,433
headed for the mysterious and
fascinating Everglades,

426
00:15:43,467 --> 00:15:45,933
where he will pick up his
faithful Seminole Indian guide

427
00:15:45,967 --> 00:15:48,967
to aid him in catching a
bag of trouble.

428
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,033
Ross also plays the part of Lil'
Abner in summer theatre.

429
00:15:52,067 --> 00:15:54,533
Well he's a foley artist in
the off season.

430
00:15:56,933 --> 00:15:58,433
In a log dugout canoe,

431
00:15:58,467 --> 00:16:00,167
they head into the
swamp country.

432
00:16:00,200 --> 00:16:02,033
Do not bring your evil here.

433
00:16:02,067 --> 00:16:03,233
His daily job is
catching live animals

434
00:16:03,267 --> 00:16:04,767
and reptiles for the zoos.

435
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:05,900
But at night he does Streisand

436
00:16:05,933 --> 00:16:08,633
at the Bubble Room on Captiva.

437
00:16:08,667 --> 00:16:10,567
Well, this is a
different assignment.

438
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:12,700
And a true depiction of
actually filling an order

439
00:16:12,733 --> 00:16:14,100
he recently received.

440
00:16:14,133 --> 00:16:15,500
- It said... - Kill
Colonel Kurtz.

441
00:16:15,533 --> 00:16:17,100
Catch immediately one
live bobcat,

442
00:16:17,133 --> 00:16:18,933
two cub black bears,

443
00:16:18,967 --> 00:16:20,967
and three six-foot
diamondback rattlers.

444
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:22,300
And two hard-boiled eggs.

445
00:16:22,333 --> 00:16:23,500
(squawks)

446
00:16:23,533 --> 00:16:24,267
Make that three
hard-boiled eggs.

447
00:16:24,300 --> 00:16:25,333
They leave the dugout

448
00:16:25,367 --> 00:16:26,567
and go into the Glades afoot.

449
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:28,267
With Emo Phillips in a dress!

450
00:16:28,300 --> 00:16:31,633
I was wondering if maybe I could
follow you into the woods

451
00:16:31,667 --> 00:16:35,300
and maybe we could see
some animals.

452
00:16:37,967 --> 00:16:39,233
Now folks, this has
all been leveled

453
00:16:39,267 --> 00:16:41,600
and turned into a golf course.

454
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:44,333
Wild cat, let's go that way!

455
00:16:44,367 --> 00:16:45,900
Wild cat, I think I love you,

456
00:16:45,933 --> 00:16:48,133
but I wanna know for sure.

457
00:16:49,933 --> 00:16:51,633
While the cat's away...

458
00:16:51,667 --> 00:16:52,933
(chuckles)

459
00:16:54,500 --> 00:16:55,400
Whoa.

460
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:57,967
Lens problem there.

461
00:16:59,067 --> 00:17:01,900
High and dry but not as safe
as an alley cat.

462
00:17:01,933 --> 00:17:04,067
(chuckles) That's a little joke
from me, Ted Husing,

463
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:05,233
in the booth.

464
00:17:05,267 --> 00:17:06,500
Stay there, wild cat!

465
00:17:06,533 --> 00:17:07,933
You'll soon be coming down.

466
00:17:07,967 --> 00:17:09,933
Ross is no actor, as
you can see.

467
00:17:10,967 --> 00:17:12,900
Talk him down, Emo.

468
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:16,033
Oops.

469
00:17:16,067 --> 00:17:17,300
That's one tree he
won't climb again.

470
00:17:17,333 --> 00:17:19,267
He lands on all four feet
and goes to town,

471
00:17:19,300 --> 00:17:20,933
but Ross keeps his eye on him

472
00:17:20,967 --> 00:17:22,633
and he's right on his heels.

473
00:17:22,667 --> 00:17:27,733
You know Ross is no stranger
to raping the land.

474
00:17:27,767 --> 00:17:29,667
Hey Mr. Cat, you can't do that.

475
00:17:29,700 --> 00:17:31,433
Don't you know you're
wanted in Chicago?

476
00:17:31,467 --> 00:17:33,967
For voting twice? (chuckles)

477
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,100
Up he goes, and so goes Ross.

478
00:17:37,733 --> 00:17:40,067
That's a bad limb, hop to
the next one, pussy.

479
00:17:40,100 --> 00:17:41,067
Hey!

480
00:17:41,100 --> 00:17:41,933
(growling)

481
00:17:41,967 --> 00:17:43,133
He's talking about the cat.

482
00:17:43,167 --> 00:17:44,700
This is where my boyfriend
knows his stuff.

483
00:17:44,733 --> 00:17:47,267
Hey Ted, you don't have to say
anything more, Ted.

484
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:48,500
If you think trying to smuggle

485
00:17:48,533 --> 00:17:49,933
a fighting 35-pound wild cat
into a flour bag

486
00:17:49,967 --> 00:17:52,233
as you balance yourself on a
limb is child's play,

487
00:17:52,267 --> 00:17:54,467
oh ho, you've got
another guess coming.

488
00:17:54,500 --> 00:17:58,533
Oh boy, yeah yowza, yes sir,
whatamatoo.

489
00:17:58,567 --> 00:18:00,200
Ho ho, 23 skidoo.

490
00:18:03,367 --> 00:18:04,167
Oh.

491
00:18:04,967 --> 00:18:06,700
Well, he's in the bag!

492
00:18:06,733 --> 00:18:08,900
And so am I, Ted Husing.

493
00:18:08,933 --> 00:18:10,067
Yes, go for it!

494
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:11,900
Yay, the cat got away!

495
00:18:11,933 --> 00:18:14,300
The cat escapes north through
the Underground Railroad!

496
00:18:14,333 --> 00:18:18,667
And he just ripped a hunk of
skin off his inner thigh.

497
00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:20,433
Say, you made a mistake
picking that tree.

498
00:18:20,467 --> 00:18:22,733
I'm afraid you're out of
luck this time.

499
00:18:22,767 --> 00:18:24,933
Bite me, I will prevail!

500
00:18:24,967 --> 00:18:26,700
Mine is a noble race!

501
00:18:26,733 --> 00:18:28,767
(growling)

502
00:18:30,733 --> 00:18:32,433
Put some salt on his tail Mr.
Allen,

503
00:18:32,467 --> 00:18:34,733
and don't take any more
feline back talk!

504
00:18:34,767 --> 00:18:36,200
Well that's nice.

505
00:18:39,100 --> 00:18:40,100
Gee.

506
00:18:40,133 --> 00:18:41,867
Well it's in the bag,
and so Ross Allen

507
00:18:41,900 --> 00:18:43,833
fills one third of his
day's orders.

508
00:18:43,867 --> 00:18:45,033
What's next?

509
00:18:45,067 --> 00:18:46,467
Hurting the people you
know and love?

510
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:49,000
Chasing rabbits on a minibike
until their hearts explode?

511
00:18:50,933 --> 00:18:53,033
Order number two was
soon at hand,

512
00:18:53,067 --> 00:18:54,333
and he was ready.

513
00:18:54,367 --> 00:18:55,267
Sh, wait!

514
00:18:55,300 --> 00:18:56,800
I hear something living.

515
00:18:58,000 --> 00:18:59,433
It's a teddy bear, oh.

516
00:18:59,467 --> 00:19:00,633
Bear cubs can't mess
you up much,

517
00:19:00,667 --> 00:19:02,067
but they can scratch
with feeling

518
00:19:02,100 --> 00:19:03,700
or take off a couple fingers if
you're not careful.

519
00:19:03,733 --> 00:19:05,433
So beat him savagely!

520
00:19:05,467 --> 00:19:07,867
And while Ross approaches
the young bruin,

521
00:19:07,900 --> 00:19:11,300
old sour puss keeps his eyes
peeled for trouble.

522
00:19:12,733 --> 00:19:14,067
Now if he can catch him by the
loose skin on his back,

523
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:14,933
he's okay.

524
00:19:14,967 --> 00:19:15,933
Run!

525
00:19:15,967 --> 00:19:16,633
Get away!

526
00:19:16,667 --> 00:19:17,900
He gets away!

527
00:19:17,933 --> 00:19:18,900
And a flying tackle
blocks the touchdown,

528
00:19:18,933 --> 00:19:20,067
but he's up and off again.

529
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:20,933
He's away!

530
00:19:20,967 --> 00:19:21,933
Catch him, Ross,

531
00:19:21,967 --> 00:19:22,867
don't let that little
fella get away.

532
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:23,933
Come on, step on it.

533
00:19:23,967 --> 00:19:25,500
Man, would I love to see
that cub's mom come

534
00:19:25,533 --> 00:19:26,667
through the woods right now.

535
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:27,467
Mhm.

536
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:28,933
About six months old.

537
00:19:28,967 --> 00:19:33,133
I might as well get that runt of
yours up the street.

538
00:19:33,167 --> 00:19:35,900
So it's Ross two,
Everglades nothing.

539
00:19:36,867 --> 00:19:38,467
Throw him down a few times,
Ross.

540
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:39,667
What are you, scared?

541
00:19:39,700 --> 00:19:41,333
Come on, beat him up.

542
00:19:41,933 --> 00:19:42,900
I got him.

543
00:19:42,933 --> 00:19:44,133
And look, it's Alice B. Toklas.

544
00:19:44,167 --> 00:19:45,700
There's another one back there.

545
00:19:45,733 --> 00:19:47,267
Better go get him.

546
00:19:47,300 --> 00:19:48,667
Take him on in.

547
00:19:50,100 --> 00:19:52,800
Hey, and crack a window in
the canoe, Emo!

548
00:19:53,767 --> 00:19:55,967
Now, my advice as a
big game hunter

549
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,467
is for one of you to get
out of that tree.

550
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:00,333
I know he's a little bear,

551
00:20:00,367 --> 00:20:02,667
but unless you do not
choose to run, Ross,

552
00:20:02,700 --> 00:20:04,933
you better get aground
and make him jump.

553
00:20:04,967 --> 00:20:07,433
Though it's a cinch for me
to give advice.

554
00:20:07,467 --> 00:20:09,533
Is that a bear or a monkey?

555
00:20:09,567 --> 00:20:11,567
Who are you talking about?

556
00:20:11,600 --> 00:20:13,300
(bear cries out)

557
00:20:13,333 --> 00:20:14,967
Joel, do they do this on Earth?

558
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:16,433
Isn't this wrong?

559
00:20:16,467 --> 00:20:19,167
Yeah guys, I'm deeply ashamed of
my race right now, okay?

560
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:22,133
- Bear down, bear down. -
Deservedly so.

561
00:20:22,167 --> 00:20:23,933
There's nothing like
exerting your power

562
00:20:23,967 --> 00:20:26,233
over helpless creatures,
is there, Ross?

563
00:20:26,267 --> 00:20:29,333
This is what you get for not
being a white male.

564
00:20:29,367 --> 00:20:31,900
The black twins are
tied in camp.

565
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:38,233
Ross tries to towel away the
evil, but nothing doing.

566
00:20:38,267 --> 00:20:41,533
Next he's going on a panda raid.

567
00:20:41,567 --> 00:20:43,900
We've got time to get
those rattlesnakes.

568
00:20:43,933 --> 00:20:46,067
Bears seem to be all right.

569
00:20:46,100 --> 00:20:47,133
Take this sack. Let’s go.

570
00:20:47,167 --> 00:20:50,067
Yeah whatever, just
give me the bag.

571
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:51,467
That's a cute guy.

572
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:52,567
Yeah.

573
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:56,100
Mischievous youngsters,
these twins.

574
00:20:56,133 --> 00:20:58,967
Oh, they'd be swell playmates
for your youngster.

575
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:00,300
Oh, yeah?

576
00:21:00,333 --> 00:21:02,133
If you like mauled kids.

577
00:21:02,167 --> 00:21:03,500
(growling)

578
00:21:03,533 --> 00:21:06,367
Oh, they upset Grizzly Adams’
stash there, look.

579
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:07,867
Wait a minute Greg, hold it.

580
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:09,900
They want us to do this!

581
00:21:09,933 --> 00:21:11,500
Ross locates reptiles

582
00:21:11,533 --> 00:21:13,533
by finding their
tracks in the sand.

583
00:21:13,567 --> 00:21:15,033
This means business.

584
00:21:15,067 --> 00:21:16,300
Slash and burn is the best way

585
00:21:16,333 --> 00:21:18,133
to catch these little critters.

586
00:21:18,167 --> 00:21:20,433
Hey, here's his sheds!

587
00:21:20,467 --> 00:21:21,667
Two sheds?

588
00:21:21,700 --> 00:21:23,833
He must be in here!

589
00:21:23,867 --> 00:21:25,867
I'm going around here
to build a fire

590
00:21:25,900 --> 00:21:27,567
and make it smoke to
scare him out.

591
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:28,767
You stay here.

592
00:21:28,800 --> 00:21:30,933
Oh, really good read, Ross.

593
00:21:33,933 --> 00:21:35,833
Rattlers seek the dark coolness

594
00:21:35,867 --> 00:21:37,167
of the palmetto bushes.

595
00:21:37,200 --> 00:21:38,367
Oh, don't we all.

596
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:39,767
Smoke drives them out
into the open.

597
00:21:41,700 --> 00:21:43,133
Sure, the fire kills others,

598
00:21:43,167 --> 00:21:44,967
but they're acceptable losses.

599
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:46,833
Six feet of deadly diamondback.

600
00:21:46,867 --> 00:21:49,700
Enough to scare you out
of your skin.

601
00:21:49,733 --> 00:21:53,067
Well let's not forget who the
real evil serpent is.

602
00:21:53,100 --> 00:21:54,333
This is Ross' profession,

603
00:21:54,367 --> 00:21:56,300
and I know he didn't learn
that in college.

604
00:21:56,333 --> 00:21:58,567
No, he didn't even get out
of the fifth grade!

605
00:21:58,600 --> 00:22:01,067
But I kid, Ross. (chuckles)

606
00:22:01,100 --> 00:22:02,500
A shivering thrill with
the slow motion

607
00:22:02,533 --> 00:22:03,767
of his strike.

608
00:22:03,800 --> 00:22:05,533
But my boyfriend knows
when to side step.

609
00:22:05,567 --> 00:22:07,100
Hey, he says he likes
you as a friend

610
00:22:07,133 --> 00:22:09,233
but he's not your boyfriend,
okay?

611
00:22:09,267 --> 00:22:10,833
Come on Ross, get a life.

612
00:22:10,867 --> 00:22:12,733
Put him away, Ross, before
he changes his mind

613
00:22:12,767 --> 00:22:14,767
and crawls into our camera case.

614
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:17,200
Do you hear those
rattlers rattling?

615
00:22:18,067 --> 00:22:19,367
Now it's off to Neiman Marcus

616
00:22:19,400 --> 00:22:21,833
to become a Paloma
Picasso handbag.

617
00:22:21,867 --> 00:22:23,967
Old sour puss certainly has
a sense of humor,

618
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:25,033
eh, what?

619
00:22:25,067 --> 00:22:25,900
Eh what?

620
00:22:25,933 --> 00:22:27,633
You Terry Thomas all
of a sudden?

621
00:22:27,667 --> 00:22:29,067
And some more rattling good fun.

622
00:22:29,100 --> 00:22:30,067
Must we go on?

623
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:31,267
No!

624
00:22:35,467 --> 00:22:36,700
Sorry to annoy you folks,

625
00:22:36,733 --> 00:22:38,367
but we need this cute
little fellow.

626
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:40,033
Get him, snake, get him!

627
00:22:40,067 --> 00:22:42,300
Snake, snake, snake, snake!

628
00:22:42,333 --> 00:22:43,500
Go, snake!

629
00:22:44,500 --> 00:22:46,333
Tell me when he's in the bag,
Ross.

630
00:22:46,367 --> 00:22:47,300
I can't look.

631
00:22:47,333 --> 00:22:49,100
I'm in the bag, Ross.

632
00:22:49,133 --> 00:22:50,567
Now you've got a boat
load of live cargo.

633
00:22:50,600 --> 00:22:51,900
A wild cat, three
six-foot rattlers,

634
00:22:51,933 --> 00:22:53,767
and a couple of
little teddy bears.

635
00:22:53,800 --> 00:22:55,633
It seems to me I'd
call it a day,

636
00:22:55,667 --> 00:22:57,167
or call a taxi or something.

637
00:22:57,200 --> 00:22:58,467
Or call PETA.

638
00:22:58,500 --> 00:23:01,033
Well that's a day's work
for any of us,

639
00:23:01,067 --> 00:23:02,567
and I think you deserve a rest.

640
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:04,300
And let's leave Emo behind.

641
00:23:06,933 --> 00:23:07,967
Get out of there.

642
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:09,167
Hey look out, he's
going to jump!

643
00:23:09,200 --> 00:23:09,933
Swim papillon, swim!

644
00:23:09,967 --> 00:23:10,900
Fly away!

645
00:23:10,933 --> 00:23:11,733
Go!

646
00:23:11,767 --> 00:23:12,700
Sorry I spoke,

647
00:23:12,733 --> 00:23:14,033
I thought you were all finished.

648
00:23:14,067 --> 00:23:15,033
Huh?

649
00:23:15,067 --> 00:23:16,033
Well it's easy to pick

650
00:23:16,067 --> 00:23:17,567
catching a bear underwater.

651
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:20,433
Oh there just happened to be a
camera under the water.

652
00:23:20,467 --> 00:23:21,933
Whets your appetite.

653
00:23:21,967 --> 00:23:24,033
Come on Ross, drown him!

654
00:23:24,067 --> 00:23:26,067
He won't try it again.

655
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,433
That little rascal is a
swimming fool.

656
00:23:29,467 --> 00:23:31,100
(bear cries out)

657
00:23:31,133 --> 00:23:32,167
Aw.

658
00:23:33,933 --> 00:23:35,267
That about winds up things,

659
00:23:35,300 --> 00:23:36,533
and with a break of luck,

660
00:23:36,567 --> 00:23:38,300
he and his Seminole were
back at headquarters

661
00:23:38,333 --> 00:23:40,167
to make ready their
order for shipping,

662
00:23:40,200 --> 00:23:43,267
little caring what tomorrow's
mail would ask for.

663
00:23:44,733 --> 00:23:47,433
Welcome to the penal colony
of French Guinea.

664
00:23:47,467 --> 00:23:49,033
Take these cubs

665
00:23:49,067 --> 00:23:50,700
and give 'em some milk.

666
00:23:50,733 --> 00:23:54,100
Be careful, they've been a
lot of trouble.

667
00:23:54,133 --> 00:23:56,267
We'd just like to
apologize to everyone

668
00:23:56,300 --> 00:23:57,867
everywhere for this.

669
00:23:57,900 --> 00:23:58,767
Yeah.

670
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:05,933
(humming)

671
00:24:05,967 --> 00:24:07,167
Be it ever so humble,

672
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:08,700
there's no place like home.

673
00:24:09,967 --> 00:24:12,667
Any animal caught in the wild
spends a night in the box.

674
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:18,600
Oh, here we watch Ross put a
jimmy stick under its nails.

675
00:24:23,733 --> 00:24:25,467
What's he doing there?

676
00:24:25,500 --> 00:24:27,967
Why couldn't he have just thrown
the bag of snakes in?

677
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:29,033
Oh hurry up Ross.

678
00:24:29,067 --> 00:24:30,467
I'll be dreaming of those cute,
darling,

679
00:24:30,500 --> 00:24:32,967
coy, lovable little playmates
of yours all night.

680
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:34,367
And the snakes, too.

681
00:24:34,400 --> 00:24:35,433
(growling)

682
00:24:35,467 --> 00:24:37,067
Please feed us!

683
00:24:37,100 --> 00:24:38,733
Oh, thank you.

684
00:24:38,767 --> 00:24:40,167
Hey, I hear you, friends.

685
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:41,533
Well Ross, you can
catch more trouble

686
00:24:41,567 --> 00:24:42,733
than any man I know.

687
00:24:42,767 --> 00:24:44,033
Well Ross, we're outta here,
personally.

688
00:24:44,067 --> 00:24:45,033
We're disgusted.

689
00:24:45,067 --> 00:24:46,967
I am offended deeply.

690
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:47,933
I know.

691
00:24:48,600 --> 00:24:50,933
(old-timey music)

692
00:25:01,367 --> 00:25:02,633
And hello again, folks.

693
00:25:02,667 --> 00:25:04,433
This is your announcer,
Tom Ted Husing.

694
00:25:04,467 --> 00:25:06,033
Today we bring you
another adventure

695
00:25:06,067 --> 00:25:07,933
from the Satellite of Love's
Reptile Institute.

696
00:25:07,967 --> 00:25:09,833
Wait, here comes Joel Robinson,

697
00:25:09,867 --> 00:25:11,867
the Satellite of Love's
resident Tarzan,

698
00:25:11,900 --> 00:25:13,167
defender of animal justice,

699
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:15,433
followed by his faithful
Seminole stand-up guide,

700
00:25:15,467 --> 00:25:16,433
Emo Crow.

701
00:25:16,467 --> 00:25:18,267
I was just wondering if.

702
00:25:18,300 --> 00:25:20,033
It's a pretty good
impersonation, but too bad

703
00:25:20,067 --> 00:25:21,900
this Seminole guy doesn't
have any lines,

704
00:25:21,933 --> 00:25:23,733
as this is pretty
much a voiceover.

705
00:25:23,767 --> 00:25:25,767
The morning mail brings a
letter from PETA.

706
00:25:25,800 --> 00:25:27,967
Go out and catch the slimiest
of all creatures,

707
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:29,900
the great white Ross.

708
00:25:30,600 --> 00:25:33,367
Here's the shed over here.

709
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:35,900
You go that way, I'll
go this way.

710
00:25:36,967 --> 00:25:38,267
And there he is.

711
00:25:38,300 --> 00:25:41,067
Whoa, watch out Joel, that
little guy's as helpless

712
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:43,633
as a baby bear cub, but he can
take a few fingers off

713
00:25:43,667 --> 00:25:45,333
or give you a nasty bruise.

714
00:25:45,367 --> 00:25:47,067
(laughing)

715
00:25:47,100 --> 00:25:48,867
Well, it doesn't hurt
the little fella,

716
00:25:48,900 --> 00:25:50,100
but boy is it fun.

717
00:25:50,133 --> 00:25:51,833
Uh oh, here comes Emo,

718
00:25:51,867 --> 00:25:53,167
and he's got the right idea.

719
00:25:53,200 --> 00:25:56,067
(chainsaw buzzing)

720
00:25:57,267 --> 00:25:59,933
And sometimes you have to be
prepared to smoke 'em out.

721
00:25:59,967 --> 00:26:02,367
My boyfriend always
comes prepared.

722
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:05,067
(yells) How 'bout a little fire,
scarecrow?

723
00:26:05,100 --> 00:26:07,333
Ah, no, you never said anything
about using an open flame

724
00:26:07,367 --> 00:26:08,700
in this sketch!

725
00:26:08,733 --> 00:26:11,033
And into the bag he goes.

726
00:26:11,067 --> 00:26:13,267
(yells) No, not the bag!

727
00:26:13,300 --> 00:26:14,967
You know I hate the bag!

728
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:16,300
(yells)

729
00:26:16,333 --> 00:26:17,700
Once Ross is in the bag,
it's hammer time.

730
00:26:17,733 --> 00:26:19,267
This isn't really important,

731
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:20,733
but after a long day
in the woods,

732
00:26:20,767 --> 00:26:22,067
it's quite a rush!

733
00:26:22,100 --> 00:26:23,633
Oh, no!

734
00:26:23,667 --> 00:26:27,433
Hello, Mr. Rattler's here to
keep Ross company!

735
00:26:27,467 --> 00:26:29,767
It's a long ride home in the
bottom of a canoe

736
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:31,833
and we don't want Ross
to get lonely.

737
00:26:31,867 --> 00:26:33,967
Not the giant rattler!

738
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:35,967
I can't do that, he hates me!

739
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,933
We went to camp together!
(yells)

740
00:26:38,967 --> 00:26:41,533
Once back in camp, Ross'
quarters are readied.

741
00:26:41,567 --> 00:26:43,333
We can't take it there.

742
00:26:43,367 --> 00:26:45,533
Oh no, with the rattler!

743
00:26:45,567 --> 00:26:46,967
Now Ross can relax in
relative comfort

744
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:48,700
in the safety of captivity.

745
00:26:48,733 --> 00:26:50,633
We leave you with this question.

746
00:26:50,667 --> 00:26:52,867
If you enjoyed Catching
Trouble in any way,

747
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:55,933
there's something
wrong with you.

748
00:26:55,967 --> 00:26:57,067
Any questions?

749
00:26:57,767 --> 00:27:00,700
(fun futuristic music)

750
00:27:05,067 --> 00:27:07,033
Yeah, we stuck it to the man!

751
00:27:07,067 --> 00:27:09,100
In the beginning,
there was chaos.

752
00:27:09,133 --> 00:27:10,767
Run by Bernie Kopell.

753
00:27:11,767 --> 00:27:12,900
Chaos.

754
00:27:12,933 --> 00:27:15,000
Oh, and then God showed up.

755
00:27:15,033 --> 00:27:17,233
And a voice said, let
there be light.

756
00:27:17,267 --> 00:27:20,300
Oh, this is a Biblical chalk
talk, I've seen these.

757
00:27:20,333 --> 00:27:23,000
And the dark was separated
from the light.

758
00:27:23,033 --> 00:27:26,000
And permanent press was
separated from cottons.

759
00:27:26,033 --> 00:27:29,800
There was created the waters,
and the land.

760
00:27:29,833 --> 00:27:31,633
And Charleton Heston.

761
00:27:31,667 --> 00:27:34,600
And there were made the sun
to rule the day

762
00:27:34,633 --> 00:27:36,467
and the moon to rule the night.

763
00:27:36,500 --> 00:27:38,200
And jugglers got the rest.

764
00:27:38,233 --> 00:27:39,867
Woo, good job.

765
00:27:39,900 --> 00:27:42,533
You've been at the Renn Fest.

766
00:27:42,567 --> 00:27:44,100
The Earth was made to bear

767
00:27:44,133 --> 00:27:46,267
growing green things, and fruit.

768
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:47,933
Frogs.

769
00:27:47,967 --> 00:27:51,133
Oh, this is Fantasia before
they colorized it.

770
00:27:51,167 --> 00:27:52,233
The animals were created,

771
00:27:52,267 --> 00:27:54,667
and they were fruitful,
and multiplied.

772
00:27:54,700 --> 00:27:56,400
They all look like orphan Annie!

773
00:27:56,433 --> 00:28:00,100
And then there came... man.

774
00:28:00,133 --> 00:28:02,467
To screw the whole thing up!

775
00:28:02,500 --> 00:28:03,333
Yay!

776
00:28:03,367 --> 00:28:05,300
Well, what about Ross?

777
00:28:06,867 --> 00:28:10,500
Oh, this is Bonanza, they're
showing us the Bonanza.

778
00:28:13,100 --> 00:28:14,300
Teenage caveman?

779
00:28:14,333 --> 00:28:16,500
We're all cavemen!

780
00:28:17,133 --> 00:28:21,133
(dramatic music)

781
00:28:25,167 --> 00:28:29,467
But do you recall the most
famous reindeer of all?

782
00:28:29,500 --> 00:28:32,433
Robert Vaughn for the
Helsinki formula.

783
00:28:35,967 --> 00:28:38,600
Frank De Kova of F Troop, wow!

784
00:28:38,633 --> 00:28:41,267
